WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
A liver cell reads cholesterol in its ER membrane.

2
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.820
When the reading falls, it makes more LDL receptors.

3
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.380
Now meet three drugs.

4
00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.860
A statin blocks the cell's own making line.

5
00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.600
The other two act in the gut.

6
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.840
How can a drug in the gut change receptors in the liver?

7
00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:26.013
Start with the drug from before.

8
00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:31.413
A statin blocks HMG-CoA reductase, the main throttle.

9
00:00:32.813 --> 00:00:36.353
Less new cholesterol reaches the ER, so the gauge reads low.

10
00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:40.493
Cholesterol also reaches the liver from the gut.

11
00:00:41.893 --> 00:00:46.013
Ezetimibe blocks cholesterol absorption, so less arrives by that route.

12
00:00:47.233 --> 00:00:50.073
A different door, yet the ER reading drops again.

13
00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:53.573
The third drug is a bile-acid resin.

14
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:57.493
The liver makes bile acids from its own cholesterol.

15
00:00:58.793 --> 00:01:00.913
The resin traps bile acids in the gut.

16
00:01:02.493 --> 00:01:05.533
Predict: what happens to cholesterol in the liver cell's ER?

17
00:01:11.617 --> 00:01:17.857
It falls. With bile acids trapped in the gut, the liver turns more of its own

18
00:01:17.857 --> 00:01:20.017
cholesterol into new bile acids.

19
00:01:21.357 --> 00:01:22.777
So ER cholesterol drops.

20
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.400
Here is the one idea.

21
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:32.180
Any drug that lowers cholesterol in the liver cell's ER tends to raise LDL

22
00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:37.140
receptors. The gauge reads the level, not where the shortage began.

23
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:42.770
Freed SREBP-2 turns on the LDL-receptor gene.

24
00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:47.350
Liver cells carry most of the body's LDL receptors.

25
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.770
More of them at the surface pull more LDL from the blood.

26
00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:56.770
This takes hours, because the gauge changes how much protein is made.

27
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.040
But the same switch turns on one more gene.

28
00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.980
It makes PCSK9, a protein the cell secretes.

29
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.020
PCSK9 binds the LDL receptor and sends it to the lysosome to be destroyed.

30
00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:15.610
Fewer receptors return to the surface.

31
00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:22.790
So a statin raises PCSK9 along with the receptors, and gives back part of its gain.

32
00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:25.590
The gauge brakes its own answer.

33
00:02:26.633 --> 00:02:31.693
Three drugs, three doors: the making line, absorption, and the bile-acid drain.

34
00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:35.053
Each one lowers the same ER pool.

35
00:02:36.313 --> 00:02:41.413
Whatever drains it, the liver tends to answer the same way, with more receptors at

36
00:02:41.413 --> 00:02:41.933
its surface.
